Kachemak Bay Watershed Council, Homer, AK.
On behalf of the Kachemak Bay Watershed Council, I am writing to oppose a preliminary permit under Docket No. P-15423-000 for a feasibility study, as well as any future seabed disturbance or physical construction of the proposed 1,650-acre DeepGreen Cook Inlet underwater hyperscale data center. There is little point in expending resources on a study for a project that should not be implemented due to a litany of environmental impacts. 

These impacts include: 

•	“Passive ocean cooling,” which would discharge immense plumes of waste heat into the water column when Cook Inlet is already stressed due to warming ocean waters and ocean acidification;

•	Constant humming from fans and pumps, which, when coupled with heavy boat traffic in the region, impedes the echolocation of the 300 endangered beluga whales and other whale species; 

•	Numerous negative impacts from anthropogenic electromagnetic fields which alter migration cues, stunt growth in larval fish and shellfish, and alter predator-prey interactions in salmon and other marine species; 

•	A migratory bottleneck for salmon due to the proposed location’s site; 

•	Lack of public outreach and consultation, including any effort to seek input from the eight federally recognized Alaska Native Tribes in the Cook Inlet region who rely on the marine ecosystem for most of their subsistence needs; 

•	And finally, due to a lack of disclosure about increased energy rates for Kenai Peninsula Borough residents.

While the Kachemak Bay Watershed Council is not opposed to responsible tidal energy to replace oil and gas production, the proposed DeepGreen project does nothing to advance that objective. 

Jessica Shepherd
Kachemak Bay Watershed Council
